# Website Brochure decision log

## 2026-11-19 — Read only the submitted page

**Decision:** Version 1 fetches and extracts readable text from only the exact page submitted by the user (plus bounded HTTP redirects). It does not crawl same-site links.

**Reason:** A single-page scope keeps provenance understandable, latency bounded, and SSRF protections auditable.

**Trade-off:** JavaScript-only pages and sparse landing pages may not provide enough source material; the app reports that limitation instead of filling gaps.

## 2026-11-19 — Use a small provider boundary with one output schema

**Decision:** OpenAI Responses and Ollama Chat are implemented behind the same `BrochureGenerator` protocol and both must return the same strict Pydantic `GeneratedContent` schema.

**Reason:** Provider selection stays entirely server-side while the route and browser receive a stable application-level result.

**Trade-off:** Provider-specific features are intentionally limited to structured-output configuration, and models that cannot reliably produce the schema are rejected.

## 2026-11-19 — Keep brochure styling in the browser

**Decision:** The model returns structured text only. App-owned HTML and CSS render three themes and an accent color selected after generation.

**Reason:** Theme changes are instant, safe to render with `textContent`, and do not refetch the page or spend another model call.

**Trade-off:** Version 1 themes use a shared layout rather than model-designed or fully custom brochure layouts.

## 2026-11-19 — Shift focus from source entry to the document

**Decision:** The first visit centers a compact source form beside a local example. After generation, the form collapses and the brochure becomes the primary workspace, with source context, styling, and print actions arranged around it. Editing explicitly restores the saved URL and category while keeping the last successful document available.

**Reason:** Entering a URL and reviewing a brochure are different tasks. Giving each state a clear visual priority reduces first-visit friction and gives the generated document enough room for meaningful review.

**Trade-off:** The frontend keeps a small amount of view state so it can preserve a successful result through replacement loading and errors. The generation API and structured content model remain unchanged.

## 2026-11-20 — Log provider-native generation metrics

**Decision:** Each provider records one structured server-side generation event with provider, model, elapsed time, success, provider-reported token counts, and prompt-cache reuse when available. Successful API responses also include that safe metrics subset for the local UI. Missing counters remain null.

**Reason:** This establishes a trustworthy observability baseline without changing the public API or introducing storage and pricing concerns.

**Trade-off:** Metrics are request-scoped rather than stored historically, and Ollama total tokens are available only when both prompt and evaluation counts are present.

## 2026-11-20 — Estimate cost only from explicit pricing

**Decision:** OpenAI cost estimates use a small, dated allowlist of standard per-token prices and distinguish regular input, cached input, and output tokens. Unknown models, incomplete usage, and Ollama return no estimate.

**Reason:** A narrow pricing table makes assumptions visible and prevents local compute or unverified model rates from appearing as API billing data.

**Trade-off:** The estimate must be reviewed when pricing changes and intentionally excludes account-specific discounts, regional uplifts, taxes, and non-token charges.

## 2026-11-21 — Compare configured models with one source fetch

**Decision:** Model comparison is an optional route and browser workspace layered on the existing provider boundary. The server fetches a page once, passes the same immutable extracted content and category to two configured generators concurrently, and returns an independent result envelope for each.

**Reason:** Reusing the provider implementations and metrics keeps the comparison meaningful and small while preserving the original single-model workflow.

**Trade-off:** Version 1 compares the one configured model per provider, stores no ratings or history, and cannot expose generation metrics for a provider call that fails before returning a successful result.

